## Name

plot_conc_time_mean


## Alias

plot_conc_time_mean


## Title

Generate Mean ± SD Concentration-Time Summary Plot


## Usage

```r
plot_conc_time_mean(
  data,
  x_var,
  y_var,
  id_var,
  color_var,
  split_by = NULL,
  facet_by = NULL,
  shape_var = NULL,
  color_limit = 8,
  stat_type = c("arithmetic", "geometric"),
  errorbar = c("upper", "both"),
  x_units = NULL,
  y_units = NULL,
  x_lab = NULL,
  y_lab = NULL,
  color_lab = NULL,
  shape_lab = NULL,
  style = pkdemo_style(),
  blq = c("drop", "zero", "half_lloq"),
  lloq = NULL,
  mean_below_lloq = c("none", "zero", "missing")
)
```

## Arguments

<div class="arg-table">
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Argument</th><th>Description</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>data</code></td>
<td>A data frame containing individual PK data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>x_var</code></td>
<td>Time variable (column name, quoted or unquoted, e.g. NTIME or "NTIME")</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>y_var</code></td>
<td>Concentration variable (column name, quoted or unquoted, e.g. AVAL)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>id_var</code></td>
<td>Subject ID variable (column name, quoted or unquoted, e.g. USUBJID)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>color_var</code></td>
<td>Grouping variable mapped to color (column name, quoted or unquoted, e.g. COHORT or "DOSE")</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>split_by</code></td>
<td>Character vector of column names used to split the data into separate plots. NULL (default) returns a single ggplot. When provided, returns a named list of ggplots, one per unique combination.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>facet_by</code></td>
<td>Optional single column name; when set, the returned plot(s) are faceted by this column via `ggplot2::facet_wrap()`. Stratifies the summary by (color_var, facet_by, x_var) so each panel shows only its own data. Independent of `split_by` (which produces separate plot objects); the two can be combined.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>shape_var</code></td>
<td>Optional single column name mapped to point shape. Useful for overlaying a secondary stratifier (e.g. visit or period) on top of the color-coded group. Stratifies the summary by that column.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>color_limit</code></td>
<td>Maximum number of `color_var` groups that colour alone encodes. When the number of groups exceeds this, groups are split into `ceiling(n / color_limit)` equal sets --- balanced rather than filled to `color_limit` each (12 groups at 8 gives 6 + 6, not 8 + 4) --- with colours repeating across sets while linetype changes per set, so each group stays distinguishable. At or below the limit, every group gets a unique colour and a solid line. Default: 8.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>stat_type</code></td>
<td>Summary statistic family. One of: - **"arithmetic"**: Arithmetic mean and SD (default) - **"geometric"**: Geometric mean and geometric SD on the log scale; non-positive y values are dropped before summarising</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>errorbar</code></td>
<td>Error bar style. One of: - **"upper"**: Upper whisker only (default) - **"both"**: Upper and lower whiskers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>x_units</code></td>
<td>Optional units string appended to the x-axis label. May also be a named list/character vector keyed by a value of one of the `split_by` columns (e.g. `c(Drug1 = "h", Drug2 = "min")`); each split picks the matching entry.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>y_units</code></td>
<td>Optional units string appended to the y-axis label. Accepts the same per-group lookup form as `x_units`.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>x_lab</code></td>
<td>Override x-axis label. If NULL, derived from x_var + x_units. Also accepts a named list/character vector keyed by a split value.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>y_lab</code></td>
<td>Override y-axis label. If NULL, derived from y_var + y_units. Same per-group lookup form as `x_lab`.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>color_lab</code></td>
<td>Legend title. Default: column name of color_var</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>shape_lab</code></td>
<td>Legend title for the shape aesthetic. Default: column name of `shape_var`.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>style</code></td>
<td>A `ggstylekit_style_spec` controlling theme, base geom looks, log axis (`logy`), and plot-level/legend appearance. Defaults to [`pkdemo_style()`](../pkdemo_style/); pass `pkdemo_style(logy = TRUE)` for a log y-axis. The plot is finished with style_plot. Named `colors` pins are keyed by `color_var` value (`c(`50` = "steelblue")`), resolved per split, and do not reach the set-based path above `color_limit`, which owns colour itself. A whole-palette choice does reach it, so `pkdemo_style(colorblind = TRUE)` recolours these plots too.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>blq</code></td>
<td>How to handle BLQ values — non-numeric strings in a character y_var column. NA rows are always dropped. One of "drop" (default), "zero", or "half_lloq"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>lloq</code></td>
<td>Scalar numeric LLOQ, or unquoted column name with per-row LLOQ values. Required when blq = "half_lloq". When `mean_below_lloq` is set, lloq must instead be a scalar numeric or a named numeric vector keyed by a `split_by` value (e.g. `c(Drug1 = 25, Drug2 = 0.5)`) — column references are not supported for that comparison.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>mean_below_lloq</code></td>
<td>How to handle per-group mean values that fall below the LLOQ. One of: - **"none"**: Leave means as computed (default) - **"zero"**: Replace mean (and its error-bar bounds) with 0 - **"missing"**: Replace mean (and its error-bar bounds) with NA, so the connecting line breaks at that point Requires `lloq`. When lloq is a named lookup, each split's mean is compared against the matching entry; splits with no matching name are left unchanged.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>


## Value

A ggplot object when `split_by` is NULL, otherwise a named list of
ggplot objects with names like "var1=val1_var2=val2".


## Description

Computes mean and standard deviation (arithmetic by default, optionally
geometric) of a concentration variable at each time point, stratified by a
grouping variable (e.g. cohort or dose level). Plots mean lines with SD
error bars; legend labels include the number of unique subjects per group
formatted as "\<group> (N = n)". For geometric statistics the error bars
span [mean / GSD, mean × GSD]. Each group gets a distinct colour; once the
number of groups exceeds `color_limit`, colours repeat across balanced
sets and linetype varies per set so groups stay distinguishable. When
`split_by` is supplied, returns one plot per unique combination of those
columns (useful for separating by analyte, cycle, etc.).
